1
00:00:00,430 --> 00:00:01,219
This is Karl.

2
00:00:01,639 --> 00:00:04,460
It's January, and his whole paycheck is already gone.

3
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:06,242
Not to rent. Not to food.

4
00:00:06,662 --> 00:00:08,926
To six tiny payments of $50.

5
00:00:09,346 --> 00:00:10,836
How did it happen? One button.

6
00:00:12,156 --> 00:00:14,725
Black Friday. 40% off everything.

7
00:00:15,145 --> 00:00:17,409
And at every checkout, the same friendly button:

8
00:00:17,829 --> 00:00:19,690
"Pay in 4. Zero interest."

9
00:00:20,110 --> 00:00:20,992
Here's your question.

10
00:00:21,412 --> 00:00:24,710
By the end of this story, how much did Karl really spend?

11
00:00:25,130 --> 00:00:27,354
Guess a number now. Write it down. Keep it.

12
00:00:28,674 --> 00:00:32,010
Karl is 26. Normal job, normal paycheck,

13
00:00:32,430 --> 00:00:34,729
one credit card he's proud of not using much.

14
00:00:35,149 --> 00:00:36,963
He's not reckless. He has a list:

15
00:00:37,383 --> 00:00:40,858
a winter jacket and a gift for his mom. About $400.

16
00:00:41,278 --> 00:00:41,975
That's the plan.

17
00:00:42,395 --> 00:00:45,948
We're going to watch two versions of Karl walk into the same Black Friday.

18
00:00:46,368 --> 00:00:47,749
Timeline A uses the button.

19
00:00:48,169 --> 00:00:50,456
Timeline B uses one strange little habit.

20
00:00:50,876 --> 00:00:53,512
Same guy, same money, same sales.

21
00:00:54,832 --> 00:00:57,366
Timeline A. The jacket is $200.

22
00:00:57,786 --> 00:00:58,912
Too much for one day.

23
00:00:59,332 --> 00:01:00,621
But the Cashier leans in.

24
00:01:01,041 --> 00:01:02,129
"Or just $50 today!"

25
00:01:02,549 --> 00:01:04,889
Fifty. Fifty is nothing. Fifty is a dinner.

26
00:01:05,309 --> 00:01:05,565
Click.

27
00:01:05,985 --> 00:01:08,539
Here's the trick, and it's not Karl being stupid.

28
00:01:08,959 --> 00:01:12,411
Your brain doesn't feel $200. It feels $50.

29
00:01:12,831 --> 00:01:16,813
Splitting a price into four makes paying hurt about four times less.

30
00:01:17,233 --> 00:01:18,920
That's not a bug. That's the product.

31
00:01:19,340 --> 00:01:24,216
Stores pay these apps a fee on every sale, because people who split the price buy more.

32
00:01:24,636 --> 00:01:28,761
One study from the U.S. National Bureau of Economic Research found

33
00:01:29,181 --> 00:01:35,187
that after people start using buy now, pay later, their spending goes up and stays up, around $60 a week.

34
00:01:36,507 --> 00:01:38,531
Then the headphones. Fifty today.

35
00:01:38,951 --> 00:01:40,487
The sneakers. Fifty today.

36
00:01:40,907 --> 00:01:43,662
A speaker he didn't know he needed. Fifty today.

37
00:01:44,082 --> 00:01:47,220
None of these feel big, because none of them are big. Alone.

38
00:01:47,640 --> 00:01:51,657
And here's the sneaky part: each app only sees its own fifty dollars.

39
00:01:52,077 --> 00:01:56,725
Nobody adds them up. Not the store, not the app, and definitely not Karl at midnight.

40
00:01:57,145 --> 00:02:00,593
By Cyber Monday, Karl has six plans in four different apps.

41
00:02:01,013 --> 00:02:02,197
And he's not unusual.

42
00:02:02,617 --> 00:02:06,777
The U.S. Consumer Financial Protection Bureau looked at real borrowers:

43
00:02:07,197 --> 00:02:10,935
63% had several of these loans open at the same time.

44
00:02:11,355 --> 00:02:13,074
A third used more than one app.

45
00:02:13,494 --> 00:02:15,955
And it peaks exactly now, during the holidays.

46
00:02:16,375 --> 00:02:21,974
Last holiday season Americans put about $20 billion on buy now, pay later online.

47
00:02:22,394 --> 00:02:24,813
Remember your guess? Here's the first real number.

48
00:02:25,233 --> 00:02:26,765
Six plans of $200.

49
00:02:27,185 --> 00:02:29,484
Karl just spent $1,200.

50
00:02:29,904 --> 00:02:31,239
Three times his list.

51
00:02:32,559 --> 00:02:34,359
And look at everything around that button.

52
00:02:34,779 --> 00:02:38,524
A timer. "Only two left." A big green "zero interest".

53
00:02:38,944 --> 00:02:42,288
All of it is built to make you decide in seconds, not days.

54
00:02:42,708 --> 00:02:44,519
Now the two myths Karl believes.

55
00:02:44,939 --> 00:02:47,207
Myth one: "It's interest-free, so it's free."

56
00:02:47,627 --> 00:02:51,818
It's free only if every single payment is on time, and only if you would have bought it anyway.

57
00:02:52,238 --> 00:02:57,013
40% off something you didn't plan to buy isn't a saving. It's 60% spent.

58
00:02:57,433 --> 00:02:59,375
Myth two: "It's not real debt."

59
00:02:59,795 --> 00:03:00,434
It is a loan.

60
00:03:00,854 --> 00:03:04,523
And for years most of these loans didn't even show up on credit reports.

61
00:03:04,943 --> 00:03:07,056
Which means nobody saw the full stack.

62
00:03:07,476 --> 00:03:09,825
Not the banks, not the apps. Not even Karl.

63
00:03:11,145 --> 00:03:14,538
Timeline B. Same jacket, same Cashier, same button.

64
00:03:14,958 --> 00:03:16,046
"Or just $50 today!"

65
00:03:16,466 --> 00:03:17,940
But this Karl has one rule.

66
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:20,914
Before he pays, he says the full price out loud.

67
00:03:21,334 --> 00:03:22,318
"Two hundred dollars."

68
00:03:22,738 --> 00:03:26,527
It sounds silly. It works, because now his brain hears the real number.

69
00:03:26,947 --> 00:03:29,934
Then a second question: would I pay $200 in cash, today?

70
00:03:30,354 --> 00:03:32,517
For the jacket, yes. He planned it.

71
00:03:32,937 --> 00:03:34,694
He pays with his debit card. Done.

72
00:03:35,114 --> 00:03:38,566
For the headphones he says it out loud: "$199."

73
00:03:38,986 --> 00:03:40,460
He hears how it sounds.

74
00:03:40,880 --> 00:03:43,562
So he leaves them in the cart for 48 hours.

75
00:03:43,982 --> 00:03:48,409
Black Friday prices aren't the last sale of the year. Most of them come back before Christmas.

76
00:03:48,829 --> 00:03:51,279
Two days later, he doesn't even remember them.

77
00:03:51,699 --> 00:03:54,980
Timeline B spends $400. The list. Nothing else.

78
00:03:56,300 --> 00:03:58,869
Let's translate this into something money hides: time.

79
00:03:59,289 --> 00:04:03,341
The average American earns about $38 an hour before tax.

80
00:04:03,761 --> 00:04:07,441
So Timeline A's $1,200 is about 32 hours of work.

81
00:04:07,861 --> 00:04:11,484
Four full days of Karl's life, for things he mostly didn't plan to buy.

82
00:04:11,904 --> 00:04:16,025
Timeline B's extra, the stuff he didn't buy, is $800.

83
00:04:16,445 --> 00:04:18,140
21 hours he gets to keep.

84
00:04:19,460 --> 00:04:20,598
Back to Timeline A.

85
00:04:21,018 --> 00:04:25,306
Every two weeks, six payments land on the same days. $300.

86
00:04:25,726 --> 00:04:26,828
On December 11th.

87
00:04:27,248 --> 00:04:28,212
On Christmas day.

88
00:04:28,632 --> 00:04:30,981
On January 8th, the same week as rent.

89
00:04:31,401 --> 00:04:33,491
And Karl is not the only one struggling.

90
00:04:33,911 --> 00:04:39,901
In a LendingTree survey, 41% of people who use these loans paid late at least once in a year.

91
00:04:40,321 --> 00:04:42,330
The latest version says 47.

92
00:04:42,750 --> 00:04:46,991
Late fees are up to $7 or $8 a time, and a missed payment can freeze the app.

93
00:04:47,411 --> 00:04:52,810
And some providers have started reporting these loans to credit bureaus, so a late payment can follow you.

94
00:04:53,230 --> 00:04:57,804
Karl has three hundred dollars due, rent next to it, and a paycheck that doesn't stretch.

95
00:04:58,224 --> 00:05:00,314
So Karl does what feels smart.

96
00:05:01,634 --> 00:05:04,895
Meanwhile, Timeline B. January 8th. Rent. That's it.

97
00:05:05,315 --> 00:05:07,103
No bubbles, no buzzing apps.

98
00:05:07,523 --> 00:05:09,485
Honestly, it's a boring January.

99
00:05:09,905 --> 00:05:15,571
Karl B didn't get the speaker or the gaming chair, and for about two days he felt like he lost Black Friday.

100
00:05:15,991 --> 00:05:17,407
But his paycheck is his again.

101
00:05:17,827 --> 00:05:22,007
And the $800 he didn't spend is still sitting there, waiting for a decision.

102
00:05:23,327 --> 00:05:26,334
He pays each $50 payment with his credit card.

103
00:05:26,754 --> 00:05:30,078
No late fees, no freezes. Problem solved, right?

104
00:05:30,498 --> 00:05:31,392
Here's the twist.

105
00:05:31,812 --> 00:05:36,735
The zero-interest loan just moved onto a card that charges about 21% a year.

106
00:05:37,155 --> 00:05:41,721
That's the U.S. average on cards that pay interest, August 2026.

107
00:05:42,141 --> 00:05:45,438
By January, the whole $1,200 is sitting on that card.

108
00:05:45,858 --> 00:05:49,573
And Karl pays the minimum, because the minimum is what the app suggests.

109
00:05:49,993 --> 00:05:54,049
His first minimum payment is only about $33. Sounds harmless.

110
00:05:54,469 --> 00:05:58,907
But about two thirds of that $33 is just interest. The debt barely moves.

111
00:06:00,227 --> 00:06:03,145
So, your guess. How much did Karl really spend?

112
00:06:03,565 --> 00:06:07,187
At the minimum payment, it takes him about 7 years to clear the card.

113
00:06:07,607 --> 00:06:09,700
Seven years. For a Black Friday.

114
00:06:10,120 --> 00:06:13,232
He pays about $1,070 in interest.

115
00:06:13,652 --> 00:06:16,477
Total: about $2,270.

116
00:06:16,897 --> 00:06:20,197
And remember the full price, before the 40% off? $2,000.

117
00:06:20,617 --> 00:06:22,707
Karl paid more than full price for a sale.

118
00:06:23,127 --> 00:06:23,940
And Timeline B?

119
00:06:24,360 --> 00:06:32,371
If he had put those $800 into the S&P 500 ten years ago, they'd be worth about $2,850 today.

120
00:06:32,791 --> 00:06:34,498
That's history, not a promise.

121
00:06:34,918 --> 00:06:39,069
But the gap is real: one Karl has a seven-year receipt, the other has money.

122
00:06:40,389 --> 00:06:43,261
To be fair: buy now, pay later is not evil.

123
00:06:43,681 --> 00:06:50,368
If you pay every installment on time, from money you already have, for something you'd buy anyway, it really is zero interest.

124
00:06:50,788 --> 00:06:54,565
Some people use it well. The trap is the stack, and the card behind it.

125
00:06:54,985 --> 00:06:59,907
And the trap is designed for exactly the moment you're tired, it's late, and the timer says nine minutes.

126
00:07:00,327 --> 00:07:03,477
So three rules. Simple enough to remember at the checkout.

127
00:07:03,897 --> 00:07:08,827
Rule one: say the full price out loud. If you wouldn't pay it in cash today, don't split it.

128
00:07:09,247 --> 00:07:12,923
Rule two: one plan at a time. Never for groceries or bills.

129
00:07:13,343 --> 00:07:15,862
If you need a second plan, you can't afford the first.

130
00:07:16,282 --> 00:07:20,500
Rule three: never pay a buy-now-pay-later loan with a credit card.

131
00:07:20,920 --> 00:07:24,232
And if you already have a stack, don't panic. Three steps, tonight.

132
00:07:24,652 --> 00:07:29,997
One: open every app and write every date and amount in one calendar. See the real total.

133
00:07:30,417 --> 00:07:35,177
Two: pay the smallest plan off first, so the number of payments shrinks fast.

134
00:07:35,597 --> 00:07:39,768
Three: no new plans until the list is empty. Delete the apps if you have to.

135
00:07:40,188 --> 00:07:42,661
This is education, not financial advice.

136
00:07:43,981 --> 00:07:45,270
So, did you guess right?

137
00:07:45,690 --> 00:07:50,175
Was your number closer to $1,200, or to $2,270? Tell me in the comments.

138
00:07:50,595 --> 00:07:53,570
And how many of these plans do you have open right now? Be honest.
